1
00:00:00,556 --> 00:00:04,686
Python is a language for giving
instructions to a computer.

2
00:00:05,201 --> 00:00:06,971
It reads almost like English.

3
00:00:07,576 --> 00:00:10,826
You write a few lines,
and the computer follows them.

4
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:14,352
A program is a list of steps.

5
00:00:14,937 --> 00:00:17,707
The computer runs them from top to bottom.

6
00:00:18,117 --> 00:00:19,567
One step at a time.

7
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:21,717
That is the whole idea.

8
00:00:23,389 --> 00:00:24,799
The first step is print.

9
00:00:25,404 --> 00:00:27,234
Print shows text on the screen.

10
00:00:27,799 --> 00:00:30,369
Write print, then the text inside quotes.

11
00:00:32,156 --> 00:00:34,106
A variable is a named box.

12
00:00:34,791 --> 00:00:37,621
You put a value inside
with an equals sign.

13
00:00:38,136 --> 00:00:40,586
Here, the box called name holds Maya.

14
00:00:42,322 --> 00:00:45,172
Once a value is in a box, you can use it.

15
00:00:45,592 --> 00:00:47,782
Print name, and Python shows Maya.

16
00:00:48,372 --> 00:00:50,442
The box keeps the value for later.

17
00:00:52,122 --> 00:00:53,792
Every value has a type.

18
00:00:54,337 --> 00:00:56,807
A whole number like fifteen is an int.

19
00:00:57,342 --> 00:00:59,532
You can add and subtract ints.

20
00:01:01,022 --> 00:01:02,292
Text is a string.

21
00:01:02,857 --> 00:01:04,567
Text goes inside quotes.

22
00:01:05,177 --> 00:01:06,867
A word in quotes is a string.

23
00:01:08,622 --> 00:01:10,512
A boolean is true or false.

24
00:01:11,082 --> 00:01:13,392
It answers a yes or no question.

25
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:16,167
Python uses it to make decisions.

26
00:01:18,056 --> 00:01:20,466
A list holds many values in order.

27
00:01:20,986 --> 00:01:22,196
The list holds mango,

28
00:01:22,846 --> 00:01:23,596
apple and banana.

29
00:01:24,261 --> 00:01:25,791
The first item is mango,

30
00:01:26,201 --> 00:01:28,031
because counting starts at zero.

31
00:01:29,722 --> 00:01:30,692
Let us pause.

32
00:01:31,397 --> 00:01:34,647
You now know print, variables,
three types and lists.

33
00:01:35,297 --> 00:01:36,207
Take a breath.

34
00:01:36,822 --> 00:01:38,192
That is a good start.

35
00:01:41,056 --> 00:01:42,806
Conditions choose a path.

36
00:01:43,356 --> 00:01:45,866
If marks are forty or more, print pass.

37
00:01:46,466 --> 00:01:48,696
If not, Python skips that line.

38
00:01:50,422 --> 00:01:51,932
Else is the other path.

39
00:01:52,552 --> 00:01:55,062
If the marks are low, print try again.

40
00:01:55,572 --> 00:01:57,162
Only one branch runs.

41
00:01:58,922 --> 00:02:00,472
A loop repeats work.

42
00:02:01,012 --> 00:02:03,982
For each number in range three,
print the number.

43
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:06,622
The output is zero, one, two.

44
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:09,177
No need to write print three times.

45
00:02:10,889 --> 00:02:13,339
Python uses spaces to group code.

46
00:02:13,889 --> 00:02:17,959
The indented line belongs to
the if or the for above it.

47
00:02:18,319 --> 00:02:20,429
The spaces are part of the syntax.

48
00:02:22,356 --> 00:02:24,806
A function is a reusable block.

49
00:02:25,341 --> 00:02:27,871
Define it once, then call it by name.

50
00:02:28,406 --> 00:02:30,601
Call greet, and it prints Hi.

51
00:02:30,851 --> 00:02:33,201
Use it again whenever you need it.

52
00:02:34,822 --> 00:02:35,812
Another pause.

53
00:02:36,457 --> 00:02:37,687
You now know conditions,

54
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:40,567
loops, indentation and functions.

55
00:02:41,252 --> 00:02:43,322
These four make programs useful.

56
00:02:46,089 --> 00:02:47,579
That is the core of Python.

57
00:02:49,214 --> 00:02:51,124
Print, variables, types,
lists, conditions,

58
00:02:51,494 --> 00:02:53,484
loops, indentation and functions.

59
00:02:54,144 --> 00:02:58,154
Take one idea at a time,
and practice a little every day.

